# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Claude Code sandbox mountpoint stubs.
#
# The Bash sandbox blocks reads of shell config and IDE paths by bind-mounting
# /dev/null over them RELATIVE TO THE WORKING DIRECTORY. A bind mount needs an
# existing mountpoint, so a nonexistent target gets an empty file created to
# mount onto; teardown leaves the stub behind. Result: every repo you run a
# sandboxed command in accumulates a fistful of 0-byte files at its root, which
# `git add -A` would happily commit.
#
# Tells that these are stubs and not real config: they appear as 0-byte files
# (including .idea and .vscode, which are normally DIRECTORIES), all created
# within milliseconds of each other, and while a sandboxed command is running
# they are live mounts — `rm` reports "Device or resource busy".
#
# Every pattern below is anchored with a leading `/` so it matches only the
# repository root, where the stubs appear. Without the anchor these would also
# swallow files/.zshrc, files/.gitconfig and friends in the dotfiles repo the
# moment you added a new one.
#
# If a repo genuinely needs to commit one of these (a team that versions
# .vscode/settings.json, say), un-ignore it in that repo's own .gitignore —
# repo-level rules take precedence over this file:  !/.vscode/
#
# TWO OF THESE ARE ALSO REAL FILES, so read the note above before debugging a
# "why won't git stage this" problem:
#
#   .gitmodules  — written by `git submodule add`. In a submodule repo you MUST
#                  put `!/.gitmodules` in that repo's .gitignore, or the add
#                  appears to work and the file never gets committed. This
#                  entry was previously left out for exactly that reason; it is
#                  listed now because the stub recurs constantly and the
#                  repo-level opt-out is the cheaper of the two failure modes.
#   .mcp.json    — Claude Code's project-scoped MCP server config. Teams that
#                  share MCP servers commit it; those repos need
#                  `!/.mcp.json` locally.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/.bashrc
/.bash_profile
/.zshrc
/.zprofile
/.profile
/.gitconfig
/.ripgreprc
/.idea
/.vscode
/.gitmodules
/.mcp.json

# The sandbox masks the whole Claude Code config surface too, so the same stubs
# appear under .claude/. Like .idea and .vscode, most of these are normally
# DIRECTORIES and show up as 0-byte files.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE ADDING PROJECT CLAUDE CONFIG: skills/, commands/, agents/
# and hooks/ are exactly the things a team does commit. Ignoring only affects
# UNTRACKED files, so an existing tracked one is unaffected — but a NEW one
# will not show in `git status` and `git add -A` will skip it. In a repo that
# shares them, put the opt-out in its own .gitignore:  !/.claude/skills/
/.claude/agents
/.claude/commands
/.claude/hooks
/.claude/launch.json
/.claude/loop.md
/.claude/output-styles
/.claude/routines
/.claude/scheduled_tasks.json
/.claude/skills
/.claude/workflows

